sec.p:
The antibacterial experiment involved seven bacterial strains, including Gram-positive strains S. aureus ATCC 12600, S. aureus ATCC 33592, and S. aureus ATCC 33591, as well as Gram-negative strains P. aeruginosa PAO1, K. quasipneumoniae, A. baumannii ATCC 19606, and A. baumannii bio-53272. All strains were cultured overnight in cation-adjusted Mueller–Hinton broth (CAMHB) at 37 °C with constant shaking at 180 rpm. The bacterial suspensions were serially diluted in fresh medium to obtain a cell density of 5 × 105 CFU/mL for subsequent experiments. The antibacterial assays were conducted in 96-well plates. The final concentration of cAMPs in a well was set to 64 μg/mL. Four experimental groups were established: (1) blank control group, with 200 μL CAMHB; 2) Positive control group, with 180 μL bacterial suspension and 20 μL ultrapure water; (3) negative control group, with 180 μL CAMHB and 20 μL cAMPs solution; and (4) experimental group, with 180 μL bacterial suspension and 20 μL cAMPs solution. The OD600 values of suspensions were measured after incubation at 37 °C for 12 h and normalized using the following formula:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{Normalized OD }= \left({\mathrm{OD}}_{\text{experimental group}} - {\mathrm{OD}}_{\text{negative control}}\right)/\left({\mathrm{OD}}_{\text{positive control}} - {\mathrm{OD}}_{\text{blank control}}\right) \times 100\%$$\end{document}Normalized OD=ODexperimental group-ODnegative control/ODpositive control-ODblank control×100%

A 50 mL aliquot of 10% sheep red blood cell suspension was centrifuged, and the supernatant was discarded. The obtained red blood cells were washed twice with physiological saline until the supernatant became clear. The red blood cells were then diluted with 250 mL of physiological saline to obtain a 2% red blood cell suspension. In separate centrifuge tubes, 400 μL of AMP solutions were taken, and then 100 μL of 2% red blood cell suspension was added to obtain a final AMP concentration of 64 μg/mL as the experimental group. A 2% Triton X-100 solution in physiological saline was used as the positive control, while physiological saline was used as the negative control. The samples were mixed thoroughly and incubated at 37 °C in a water bath for 1 h. After incubation, the samples were centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 10 min at room temperature. Subsequently, 200 μL of supernatants was transferred to a 96-well plate, and their OD values were measured at 595 nm using a microplate reader. The hemolysis rate for each sample was then calculated as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{Hemolysis }= \left({\mathrm{absorbance}}_{\mathrm{experimental}} - {\mathrm{absorbance}}_{\mathrm{negative}}\right)/\left({\mathrm{absorbance}}_{\mathrm{positive}} - {\mathrm{absorbance}}_{\mathrm{negative}}\right) \times 100\%$$\end{document}Hemolysis=absorbanceexperimental-absorbancenegative/absorbancepositive-absorbancenegative×100%
